tion, beats everything.\n        13. Quadplex set: a quad with two single cards OR two pairs attached.\n        Use the EXACT card symbols provided in the Valid Moves list.\n        Prepare a valid JSON object like {"cards": ["♠3", "♥3"]} for your answer, along with your explanation and reason. If you pass, output: {"cards": []}\n        \n        Now is your turn, to your best ability, choose your move to maximize your chances of winning.\n        Please briefly evaluate a few proimsing moves, provide reason for your final choice, and output the action with specified format.\n        Your response format should be:\n        Evaluation: [Your Evaluation]\n        Reason: [Your Reason]\n        Answer: [Your Answer]\n    ', 'user_prompt': "You are playing as a peasant. Your aim is to be the play out all your cards before the landlord does, OR help your teammate (the other peasant) play out their cards before the landlord does. If the landlord plays out all cards first, you lose.\nYou are a Peasant. The Previous Player (Left) is the Landlord (ENEMY). The Next Player (Right) is your TEAMMATE (Peasant). You must cooperate with your teammate to beat the Landlord. The objective is to empty your hand before the landlord does, or help your teammate empty their hand before the landlord does.\n\nYour Hand: ♦10 ♥Q ♠A ♠2\nLast Play to Beat: None (Free Play)\n--- Game History (Recent) ---\nTurn 7 (You): Pass\nTurn 8 (Next Player): Pass\nTurn 8 (Prev Player): ♣A\nTurn 8 (You): RedJoker\nTurn 9 (Next Player): Pass\nTurn 9 (Prev Player): Pass\nTurn 9 (You): ♦5 ♠7 ♥7 ♣7\nTurn 10 (Next Player): Pass\nTurn 10 (Prev Player): Pass\nTurn 10 (You): ♣6 ♥9 ♣9 ♦9\nTurn 11 (Next Player): Pass\nTurn 11 (Prev Player): Pass\nTurn 11 (You): ♣J ♦J\nTurn 12 (Next Player): Pass\nTurn 12 (Prev Player): Pass\n--- Player Info ---\nCards Held by Next Player (Right) [Peasant (Teammate)]: 6\nCards Held by Prev Player (Left) [Landlord (Opponent)]: 1\n--- Cards Remaining Unknown (Card Counting) ---\n(Format explanation: 'Rank: Count', e.g., '2: 4' means there are four 2s left unknown)\nBlackJoker: 1, 2: 1, A: 1, K: 1, J: 1, 10: 1, 4: 1\nValid Moves:\nsingle: ♦10\nsingle: ♥Q\nsingle: ♠A\nsingle: ♠2\n\nSelect the best move from the Valid Moves list above. You can choose to pass even if you have valid moves that can beat the previous play, but you cannot pass if you are in a Free Play (no last play).\nEnsure your response matches one of the valid moves exactly.\nRemember to provide Explanation, Reason, and Answer.", 'reasoning': "**Analyzing the Endgame: Fight the Landlord**\n\nOkay, so I'm a peasant, and the Landlord only has one card left. This is a critical situation. I'm on the board right now, I have a 10, Q, Ace, and a 2. My teammate has six cards, and the Landlord has just one card. The goal is clear: prevent the Landlord from playing their last card and try to empty my hand to help my teammate win.\n\nI need to analyze my options carefully. Playing a low single like the 10 or Q would be suicide – if the Landlord has anything higher, they win. I've got to play my highest card, which is the 2. The unknown cards that are out there include a Black Joker, another 2, an Ace, a King, and a Jack.\n\nIf the Landlord has the Black Joker, I'm toast no matter what I do, unless they have a small card. Otherwise, if they have the other 2 or anything else lower, I win. The key is to control the board. Playing the 2 first forces the Landlord to respond. If they pass, I'll go next.\n\nSo, I'm going to play the 2, then Ace, then Queen, then 10. That forces the Landlord to respond, and I clear my cards. The best play is playing the 2 first, because playing the 2 beats everything except the Black Joker. In other words, I'm giving my teammate the best chance to win.\n\n\n", 'output': 'Evaluation: The Landlord has only one card remaining, which poses an immediate threat of losing the game if they get to play. I hold four single cards: 10, Q, A, 2. To maximize the chances of winning, I must prevent the Landlord from playing their last card. Playing a low card (10 or Q) gives the Landlord a high probability of winning immediately (e.g., with a K, A, 2, or Joker). Playing the ♠2 is the safest move; it can only be beaten by the Black Joker. If the Landlord has the Black Joker, they win regardless of my play. If they do not, the ♠2 ensures I retain the lead, allowing me to continue playing my cards from highest to lowest (A, then Q, then 10), thereby minimizing the Landlord\'s opportunities to go out.\n\nReason: The Landlord has 1 card left. I must play my highest single (2) to block them. If I play a smaller card, they might beat it and win. Playing the 2 forces the game: either they have the Black Joker and win, or they pass and I continue to control the board with my Ace.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♠2"]}'}
